Z||000005||Toddster||06-17-2001||09:10 PM||||they gotta be replaced Moocow, no choice.<P>get a door panel removal tool. It's cheap and you will be glad yougot it if youhave ever tried to remove the inside door panel with just a screwdriver before. Take the inner panel off, 5 minute job, then the lock is easy to get to. It's not too difficult an operation.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tweek||06-17-2001||09:13 PM||||I saw Phils car yesterday... Suh-weeet! It DESERVED to be in Hot Rod mag..||||reg||1
Z||000007||v8fieroGT||06-17-2001||10:12 PM||||Moocow, my 84 did that on both locks and i thought i would have to get new ones but they don't. The drivers side i took out cleaned lubricated it by putting never seaze on it and the passanger side i just took wd40 with straw and sprayed the crap out of it. Then i just kept working the key in it and now they both work perfect. So try WD40 before anything else. Its worth a shot.||||reg||1
Z||000008||v8fieroGT||06-17-2001||10:15 PM||||I just saw that you said that you tried WD40. Didnt read it all the way. But still try taking it appart and putting never seaze on it.||||reg||1
Z||000009||pHoOl||06-17-2001||10:26 PM||||I had the same exact problem and thought i was going to have to replace them.  What i ended up using was a product that we had around called CRC.  From what i hear, if you are ever going to work on an old car, you should have a bottle.  Anyway..... i squirted the $hit out of the lock, and then worked the key in and out, and tried to lock and unlock it and such.  After about 10 seconds, good as new.<P>Also, the symptoms that i was having was that it would take a lot of effort to get my key out, i would always have to jiggle it.  And it was hard at first to turn the lock.  I'd say give it a try, i think the problem is that the tumblers get dirty.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||MooCow||06-18-2001||11:07 PM||||Well I guess when I have a couple of hours I will pull it out and look at it to see if I can get it working again.  If not I will just buy one.  Thanks for helping me out.<P>                              Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||14
Z||000011||FieroJoe||06-18-2001||11:13 PM||||On my sister's blazer we had to replace the lock becuase it was seized up.  When I took it to the lock shop for the guy to rekey a new one, he took apart the old on and showed me the inside of it.  It was absolutly full of dirt and salt deposits.  No wonder it wouldnt turn!<P>He told me that most people dont realize that they should use a lubricant like Lock-eez every once and a while on cars they drive in the winter.<BR>||||reg||1
